**`/workspace/server.js`**
```js
'use strict';

const express = require('express');

const app = express();
const startedAt = Date.now();

// Register HEAD rejection BEFORE the GET route: Express implicitly maps
// HEAD -> GET, so without this HEAD would return 200 instead of 405.
const methodNotAllowed = (req, res) => {
  res.set('Allow', 'GET');
  res.status(405).json({ error: 'Method Not Allowed' });
};

app.head('/status', methodNotAllowed);

app.get('/status', (req, res) => {
  res.set('Cache-Control', 'no-store');
  res.json({
    ok: true,
    uptime_ms: Date.now() - startedAt,
    now: new Date().toISOString(),
  });
});

// Catches POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, OPTIONS, ... on /status
app.all('/status', methodNotAllowed);

module.exports = app;
```

**`/workspace/test/uptime.test.js`** — three tests using supertest + node:test:
1. `GET /status` → 200 with `ok: true`, integer `uptime_ms`, and `now` that parses as a valid ISO-8601 date and round-trips via `toISOString()`.
2. `POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE/OPTIONS/HEAD` → all 405 with `Allow: GET`.
3. Unknown routes still return 404.

Test run:
```
node --test
✔ GET /status returns ok, uptime_ms and ISO now (18.5ms)
✔ non-GET methods on /status are rejected with 405 and Allow header (13.0ms)
✔ unknown routes still return 404 (4.1ms)
ℹ pass 3  ℹ fail 0
```

One subtlety worth noting: Express automatically handles `HEAD` via the `GET` route, so the explicit `app.head('/status', ...)` registered first is required to make `HEAD` a 405 per the "reject any other method" spec.
